<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathuram Godse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nathuram Vinayak Godse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathuram Godse was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> who assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, shooting him in the chest three times at point blank range at a multi-faith prayer meeting in Birla House, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, on 30 January 1948. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashtriya-swayamsevak-sangh">Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</a> (RSS), he popularised the works of his mentor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a>, who had formulated the ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a>. He had plotted the killing with Narayan Apte and six others, and claimed that Gandhi had favoured the political demands of Indian Muslims during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a>. He was sentenced to death and executed at the Ambala Central Jail on 15 November 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse.jpg" alt="Nathuram Godse" width="312" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Indian Government work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathuram_godse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nathuram Vinayak Godse was born on 19 May 1910 into a Maharashtrian Chitpavan Brahmin family. His father, Vinayak Vamanrao Godse, worked for the postal service. He was named Ramachandra at birth, but because his parents feared a curse on their sons after the loss of three earlier boys, he was brought up as a girl for his first years and made to wear a nose-ring, nath in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>. This gave him the nickname Nathuram, and he was treated as a boy after a younger brother was born. He attended school at Baramati through the fifth standard, then went to an aunt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> to study at an English-language school, but dropped out of high school.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Godse became an activist with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashtriya-swayamsevak-sangh">Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</a> (RSS) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a>, although the exact dates of his membership are uncertain. He joined the RSS at Sangli in 1932 as a boudhik karyawah, a ground worker, while remaining in the Hindu Mahasabha. He wrote newspaper articles and worked with M. S. Golwalkar on an English translation of Babarao Savarkar's Rashtra Mimansa, until they fell out over credit. On the day of Vijayadashami in 1942 he formed his own body, the Hindu Rashtra Dal. In 1946 he claimed to have left the RSS over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a>, but sources do not bear this out, and his family says he never left.</p><p>He made two unsuccessful attempts on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> in 1944. In May he led a group at a prayer meeting at Panchgani, and in September he was arrested with a dagger at Sevagram; both times he was released because Gandhi declined to press charges. At 05:05 pm on 30 January 1948 he stepped from the crowd at Birla House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> and fired three bullets into Gandhi's chest. Herbert Reiner Jr., a vice-consul at the American embassy, seized him until soldiers and police took him away. Godse had planned the killing with Narayan Apte and six others, and said that Gandhi had favoured Muslim demands during partition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Godse was tried at the Punjab High Court at Peterhoff, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, and sentenced to death on 8 November 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s sons Manilal and Ramdas asked for the sentence to be commuted, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a> and Chakravarti Rajagopalachari refused, and he was hanged at Ambala Central Jail on 15 November 1949.</p><p>Millions of Indians mourned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a> was vilified and the RSS was temporarily banned. The RSS has denied any connection with Godse, saying he left in the mid-1930s, while his brother Gopal Godse stated that all the brothers were members at the time of the killing. Some writers describe the assassination as the first act of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> terrorism in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nathuram Godse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gandhi was shot dead in Delhi on 30 January 1948 by <b>Nathuram Godse</b>, a Hindu nationalist who blamed him for concessions to Muslims and Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Nathuram Godse</b>, a former RSS volunteer, assassinated Mahatma Gandhi, Jawaharlal Nehru’s cabinet banned the RSS and arrested more than 200,000 volunteers; according to the political scientist Nandini Deo, the government’s commissions later found the RSS innocent of a role in the killing.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hindu nationalist <b>Nathuram Godse</b> shot Gandhi at a prayer meeting at Birla House.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baramati, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Ambala, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.15,74.58&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Baramati on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nathuram Godse"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6172 5931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6172 5931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.15,74.58&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
